solution:
Let \(a,b,c>0\).  We will show
\[
\frac{1}{a^3+b^3+abc}
+\frac{1}{b^3+c^3+abc}
+\frac{1}{c^3+a^3+abc}
\;\le\;
\frac1{abc}.
\]

1.  Factor \(a^3+b^3\) and bound it from below.

Since
\[
a^3+b^3 \;=\;(a+b)\,(a^2 - ab + b^2),
\]
and by the trivial inequality \((a-b)^2\ge0\) we have
\[
a^2 + b^2 \;\ge\;2ab
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
a^2 - ab + b^2 \;\ge\;ab,
\]
it follows that
\[
a^3 + b^3 \;=\;(a+b)(a^2 - ab + b^2)\;\ge\;(a+b)\,ab.
\]
Hence
\[
a^3 + b^3 + abc
\;\ge\;
(ab)(a+b) \;+\; abc
\;=\;
ab\,(a+b+c).
\]
By exactly the same reasoning,
\[
b^3 + c^3 + abc \;\ge\; bc\,(a+b+c),
\quad
c^3 + a^3 + abc \;\ge\; ca\,(a+b+c).
\]

2.  Invert and sum.

Since for positive \(X\) and \(Y\) the inequality \(X\ge Y\) implies \(\tfrac1X\le\tfrac1Y\), we get
\[
\frac{1}{a^3+b^3+abc}
\;\le\;
\frac{1}{ab\,(a+b+c)},
\]
and similarly for the other two cyclic terms.  Therefore
\[
\frac{1}{a^3+b^3+abc}
+\frac{1}{b^3+c^3+abc}
+\frac{1}{c^3+a^3+abc}
\;\le\;
\frac{1}{ab(a+b+c)}
+\frac{1}{bc(a+b+c)}
+\frac{1}{ca(a+b+c)}.
\]
Factor out the common \(\tfrac1{a+b+c}\):
\[
\frac{1}{ab(a+b+c)}
+\frac{1}{bc(a+b+c)}
+\frac{1}{ca(a+b+c)}
\;=\;
\frac{1}{a+b+c}
\Bigl(\frac1{ab}+\frac1{bc}+\frac1{ca}\Bigr).
\]

3.  Simplify the remaining sum.

Observe that
\[
\frac1{ab} + \frac1{bc} + \frac1{ca}
\;=\;
\frac{c}{abc} + \frac{a}{abc} + \frac{b}{abc}
\;=\;
\frac{a+b+c}{abc}.
\]
Hence
\[
\frac{1}{a+b+c}
\Bigl(\frac1{ab}+\frac1{bc}+\frac1{ca}\Bigr)
\;=\;
\frac{1}{a+b+c}\,\frac{a+b+c}{abc}
\;=\;
\frac1{abc}.
\]

Combining these estimates we obtain exactly
\[
\frac{1}{a^3+b^3+abc}
+\frac{1}{b^3+c^3+abc}
+\frac{1}{c^3+a^3+abc}
\;\le\;
\frac1{abc},
\]
as required.

For each element \(x\in A\), define its “replication number”
\[
r_x \;=\; \#\bigl\{\,i : x\in A_i\bigr\}.
\]
We first show that
\[
\sum_{x\in A} r_x \;=\; 11\cdot 45
\quad\text{and}\quad
\sum_{x\in A} \binom{r_x}{2} \;=\;\binom{11}{2}\,9,
\]
and then deduce the desired bound on \(\bigl|\bigcup_iA_i\bigr|\).

1.  Since each \(A_i\) has size 45,
\[
\sum_{x\in A} r_x
\;=\;
\sum_{x\in A}\sum_{i=1}^{11} \mathbf{1}_{\{x\in A_i\}}
\;=\;
\sum_{i=1}^{11}\sum_{x\in A} \mathbf{1}_{\{x\in A_i\}}
\;=\;
\sum_{i=1}^{11} |A_i|
\;=\;
11\cdot 45 \;=\; 495.
\]

2.  Likewise, for the number of pairs \((i,j)\) with \(i<j\) that contain \(x\),
\[
\sum_{x\in A} \binom{r_x}{2}
\;=\;
\sum_{x\in A}\sum_{1\le i<j\le 11}
   \mathbf{1}_{\{x\in A_i\cap A_j\}}
\;=\;
\sum_{1\le i<j\le 11}\sum_{x\in A}
   \mathbf{1}_{\{x\in A_i\cap A_j\}}
\;=\;
\sum_{1\le i<j\le 11}\bigl|A_i\cap A_j\bigr|
\;=\;
\binom{11}{2}\,9
\;=\;
55\cdot 9
\;=\;
495.
\]

3.  From
\(\binom{r_x}{2}=\tfrac12(r_x^2-r_x)\) we get
\[
\sum_{x\in A} \bigl(r_x^2-r_x\bigr)
\;=\;
2\sum_{x\in A}\binom{r_x}{2}
\;=\;
2\cdot 495
\;=\;
990.
\]
Hence
\[
\sum_{x\in A} r_x^2
\;=\;
990 \;+\;\sum_{x\in A} r_x
\;=\;
990 \;+\;495
\;=\;
1485.
\]

4.  Let
\[
U
\;=\;
A_1\cup A_2\cup\cdots\cup A_{11},
\]
and write \(N=|U|\).  On \(U\) we have \(r_x\ge1\), while on \(A\setminus U\) we have \(r_x=0\).  Therefore
\[
\sum_{x\in A}r_x
\;=\;
\sum_{x\in U}r_x
\quad\text{and}\quad
\sum_{x\in A}r_x^2
\;=\;
\sum_{x\in U}r_x^2.
\]
By the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality,
\[
\Bigl(\sum_{x\in U}r_x\Bigr)^2
\;\le\;
\Bigl(\sum_{x\in U}1\Bigr)\,\Bigl(\sum_{x\in U}r_x^2\Bigr)
\;=\;
N\;\bigl(1485\bigr).
\]
But \(\sum_{x\in U}r_x=495\).  Hence
\[
495^2
\;\le\;
1485\,N
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
N\;\ge\;\frac{495^2}{1485}
\;=\;\frac{495}{3}
\;=\;165.
\]
Thus
\[
\bigl|A_1\cup\cdots\cup A_{11}\bigr|
\;=\;
N
\;\ge\;
165,
\]
as claimed.

Remark on equality.  Equality in Cauchy–Schwarz forces \(r_x\) to be constant (and nonzero) on \(U\).  Since \(\sum_{x\in U}r_x=495\) and \(|U|=165\), one must have \(r_x\equiv 3\) for all \(x\in U\).  In particular each element of \(U\) lies in exactly three of the \(A_i\), and each pair \(A_i,A_j\) intersects in
\[
\sum_{x\in U}\mathbf{1}_{\{x\in A_i\cap A_j\}}
\;=\;
\sum_{x\in U}\binom{r_x}{2}\Big/\binom{3}{2}
\;=\;
\frac{495}{55}
\;=\;
9.
\]

Example achieving equality.  Let
\[
U
\;=\;
\bigl\{\{i,j,k\}:1\le i<j<k\le 11\bigr\},
\]
so \(|U|=\binom{11}{3}=165\).  Adjoin to \(U\) any disjoint set \(D\) of 60 “dummy’’ points, and set
\[
A \;=\; U\;\cup\; D
\;\quad(\text{so }|A|=225).
\]
For each \(1\le i\le11\), define
\[
A_i
\;=\;
\bigl\{\,X\in U : i\in X\bigr\}.
\]
Then \(|A_i|=\binom{10}{2}=45\), and for \(i<j\),
\[
A_i\cap A_j
\;=\;
\bigl\{\,X\in U : \{i,j\}\subset X\bigr\}
\;
\Longrightarrow
\;
|A_i\cap A_j|
=\binom{9}{1}=9.
\]
Moreover \(\bigcup_{i=1}^{11}A_i=U\) has size 165, so this construction shows the bound is sharp.

problem:
Prove that the system of equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
x^{6}+x^{3}+x^{3} y+y & =147^{157} \\
x^{3}+x^{3} y+y^{2}+y+z^{9} & =157^{147}
\end{aligned}
$$

has no integer solutions.

solution:
Answer: no.

Let the points be denoted by $1,2, \ldots, 2001$ such that $i, j$ are neighbors if $|i-j|=1$ or $\{i, j\}=\{1,2001\}$. We say that $k$ points form a monochromatic segment of length $k$ if the points are consecutive on the circle and if
they all have the same color. For a coloring $F$ let $d(F)$ be the maximum length of a monochromatic segment. Note that $d\left(F_{n}\right)>1$ for all $n$ since $2001$ is odd. If $d\left(F_{1}\right)=2001$ then all points have the same color, hence $F_{1}=F_{2}=F_{3}=\ldots$ and we can choose $n_{0}=1$. Thus, let $1<d\left(F_{1}\right)<2001$. Below we shall prove the following implications:

If $3<d\left(F_{n}\right)<2001$, then $d\left(F_{n+1}\right)=d\left(F_{n}\right)-2$;
If $d\left(F_{n}\right)=3$, then $d\left(F_{n+1}\right)=2$;
If $d\left(F_{n}\right)=2$, then $d\left(F_{n+1}\right)=d\left(F_{n}\right)$ and $F_{n+2}=F_{n}$;
From (1) and (2) it follows that $d\left(F_{1000}\right) \leqslant 2$, hence by (3) we have $F_{1000}=F_{1002}$. Moreover, if $F_{1}$ is the coloring where $1$ is colored red and all other points are colored green, then $d\left(F_{1}\right)=2000$ and thus $d\left(F_{1}\right)>d\left(F_{2}\right)>\ldots>d\left(F_{1000}\right)=2$ which shows that, for all $n<1000, F_{n} \neq F_{n+2}$ and thus $1000$ cannot be replaced by $999$.
It remains to prove (1)-(3). Let $(i+1, \ldots, i+k)$ be a longest monochromatic segment for $F_{n}$ (considering the labels of the points modulo 2001). Then $(i+2, \ldots, i+k-1)$ is a monochromatic segment for $F_{n+1}$ and thus $d\left(F_{n+1}\right) \geqslant d\left(F_{n}\right)-2$. Moreover, if $(i+1, \ldots, i+k)$ is a longest monochromatic segment for $F_{n+1}$ where $k \geqslant 3$, then $(i, \ldots, i+k+1)$ is a monochromatic segment for $F_{n}$. From this and $F_{n+1}>1$ the implications (1) and (2) clearly follow. For proof of (3) note that if $d\left(F_{n}\right) \leqslant 2$ then $F_{n+1}$ is obtained from $F_{n}$ by changing the colour of all points.

First we prove inductively that for $n \geq 1, a_{n}=2^{2^{n-2}}$. We have $a_{1}=2^{2^{-1}}$, $a_{2}=2^{2^{0}}$ and

$$
a_{n+1}=2^{2^{n-2}} \cdot\left(2^{2^{n-3}}\right)^{2}=2^{2^{n-2}} \cdot 2^{2^{n-2}}=2^{2^{n-1}}
$$

Since $1+a_{1}=1+\sqrt{2}$, we must prove, that

$$
\left(1+a_{2}\right)\left(1+a_{3}\right) \cdots\left(1+a_{n}\right)<2 a_{2} a_{3} \cdots a_{n}
$$

The right-hand side is equal to

$$
2^{1+2^{0}+2^{1}+\cdots+2^{n-2}}=2^{2^{n-1}}
$$
and the left-hand side
\$\$\begin{aligned}

\left(1+2^{2^{0}}\right) \& \left(1+2^{2^{1}}\right) \cdots\left(1+2^{2^{n-2}}\right) 
\& =1+2^{2^{0}}+2^{2^{1}}+2^{2^{0}+2^{1}}+2^{2^{2}}+\cdots+2^{2^{0}+2^{1}+\cdots+2^{n-2}} 
\& =1+2+2^{2}+2^{3}+\cdots+2^{2^{n-1}-1} 
\& =2^{2^{n-1}}-1

\end{aligned}\$\$

The proof is complete.

Let $S$ be the unit circle in the $x y$-plane with origin $O$, put $A=(1,0), B=(-1,0)$, take $L$ as the line $x=1$, and suppose $X=(1,2 p)$ and $Y=(1,-2 q)$, where $p$ and $q$ are positive real numbers with $p q=\frac{c}{4}$. If $\alpha=\angle A B P$ and $\beta=\angle A B Q$, then $\tan \alpha=p$ and $\tan \beta=q$.

Let $P Q$ intersect the $x$-axis in the point $R$. By the Inscribed Angle Theorem, $\angle R O P=2 \alpha$ and $\angle R O Q=2 \beta$. The triangle $O P Q$ is isosceles, from which $\angle O P Q=\angle O Q P=90^{\circ}-\alpha-\beta$, and $\angle O R P=90^{\circ}-\alpha+\beta$. The Law of Sines gives

$$
\frac{O R}{\sin \angle O P R}=\frac{O P}{\sin \angle O R P}
$$
which implies
$$
\begin{aligned}

O R & =\frac{\sin \angle O P R}{\sin \angle O R P}=\frac{\sin \left(90^{\circ}-\alpha-\beta\right)}{\sin \left(90^{\circ}-\alpha+\beta\right)}=\frac{\cos (\alpha+\beta)}{\cos (\alpha-\beta)} 
& =\frac{\cos \alpha \cos \beta-\sin \alpha \sin \beta}{\cos \alpha \cos \beta+\sin \alpha \sin \beta}=\frac{1-\tan \alpha \tan \beta}{1+\tan \alpha \tan \beta} 
& =\frac{1-p q}{1+p q}=\frac{1-\frac{c}{4}}{1+\frac{c}{4}}=\frac{4-c}{4+c}

\end{aligned}
$$

Hence the point $R$ lies on all lines $P Q$.

