solution:
The substitution $a_{2}=\frac{x_{2}}{x_{1}}, a_{3}=\frac{x_{3}}{x_{2}}, \ldots, a_{n}=\frac{x_{1}}{x_{n-1}}$ transforms the original problem into the inequality

$$
\left(x_{1}+x_{2}\right)^{2}\left(x_{2}+x_{3}\right)^{3} \cdots\left(x_{n-1}+x_{1}\right)^{n}>n^{n} x_{1}^{2} x_{2}^{3} \cdots x_{n-1}^{n}
$$
for all $x_{1}, \ldots, x_{n-1}>0$. To prove this, we use the AM-GM inequality for each factor of the left-hand side as follows:
\$\$\begin{array}{rlll}

\left(x_{1}+x_{2}\right)^{2} \& \& \& \geq 2^{2} x_{1} x_{2} 
\left(x_{2}+x_{3}\right)^{3} \& = \& \left(2\left(\frac{x_{2}}{2}\right)+x_{3}\right)^{3} \& \geq 3^{3}\left(\frac{x_{2}}{2}\right)^{2} x_{3} 
\left(x_{3}+x_{4}\right)^{4} \& = \& \left(3\left(\frac{x_{3}}{3}\right)+x_{4}\right)^{4} \& \geq 4^{4}\left(\frac{x_{3}}{3}\right)^{3} x_{4} 
\& \vdots \& \vdots \& \vdots 
\left(x_{n-1}+x_{1}\right)^{n} \& = \& \left((n-1)\left(\frac{x_{n-1}}{n-1}\right)+x_{1}\right)^{n} \& \geq n^{n}\left(\frac{x_{n-1}}{n-1}\right)^{n-1} x_{1} .

\end{array}\$\$

Multiplying these inequalities together gives $(*)$, with inequality sign $\geq$ instead of $>$. However for the equality to occur it is necessary that $x_{1}=x_{2}, x_{2}=2 x_{3}, \ldots, x_{n-1}=(n-1) x_{1}$, implying $x_{1}=(n-1)!x_{1}$. This is impossible since $x_{1}>0$ and $n \geq 3$. Therefore the inequality is strict.

Comment. One can avoid the substitution $a_{i}=x_{i} / x_{i-1}$. Apply the weighted AM-GM inequality to each factor $\left(1+a_{k}\right)^{k}$, with the same weights like above, to obtain

$$
\left(1+a_{k}\right)^{k}=\left((k-1) \frac{1}{k-1}+a_{k}\right)^{k} \geq \frac{k^{k}}{(k-1)^{k-1}} a_{k}
$$

Multiplying all these inequalities together gives

$$
\left(1+a_{2}\right)^{2}\left(1+a_{3}\right)^{3} \cdots\left(1+a_{n}\right)^{n} \geq n^{n} a_{2} a_{3} \cdots a_{n}=n^{n}
$$

The same argument as in the proof above shows that the equality cannot be attained.

Let \(r\in\mathbb{Q}\) satisfy \(0<r<1\).  Write
\[
r=\frac{p}{q}
\]
in lowest terms, so \(p\) and \(q\) are positive integers with \(\gcd(p,q)=1\) and \(p<q\).  Define the “size” of \(r\) by
\[
\mu(r)\;=\;p+q\,.
\]
We shall prove by strong induction on \(\mu(r)\) that \(r\in S\).

Base Case (\(\mu(r)=3\)).  Since \(p\) and \(q\) are positive, \(p<q\), and \(p+q=3\), the only possibility is \(p=1\), \(q=2\), hence \(r=\tfrac12\).  By hypothesis (i), \(\tfrac12\in S\).  

Inductive Step.  Fix \(n>3\), and assume as induction hypothesis that every rational \(\rho\in(0,1)\) with \(\mu(\rho)<n\) belongs to \(S\).  Let \(r=p/q\) be any rational in \((0,1)\) with \(\mu(r)=p+q=n\).  If \(r=\tfrac12\) then we are done, so assume \(r\neq\tfrac12\); then \(q\neq2p\), and hence exactly one of the following two cases occurs.

Case 1: \(q>2p\).  
Define
\[
x \;=\;\frac{p}{\,q-p\,}\,.
\]
Since \(\gcd(p,q-p)=\gcd(p,q)=1\) (by the Euclidean algorithm), the fraction \(x\) is in lowest terms, and
\[
\mu(x)\;=\;p+(q-p)\;=\;q\;<\;p+q\;=\;n.
\]
Also \(0<x<1\) because \(p<q-p\iff q>2p\).  By the induction hypothesis, \(x\in S\).  Then by closure property (ii),
\[
\frac{x}{x+1}
\;=\;
\frac{\tfrac{p}{q-p}}{\tfrac{p}{q-p}+1}
\;=\;
\frac{p}{(q-p)+p}
\;=\;
\frac{p}{q}
\;=\;
r,
\]
so \(r\in S\).

Case 2: \(q<2p\).  
Define
\[
y \;=\;\frac{q-p}{\,p\,}\,.
\]
Again \(\gcd(q-p,p)=\gcd(q,p)=1\), so \(y\) is in lowest terms, and
\[
\mu(y)\;=\;(q-p)+p\;=\;q\;<\;n,
\]
while \(0<y<1\) because \(q-p<p\iff q<2p\).  By the induction hypothesis \(y\in S\), and then by (ii)
\[
\frac{1}{y+1}
\;=\;
\frac{1}{\tfrac{q-p}{p}+1}
\;=\;
\frac{1}{\tfrac{q}{p}}
\;=\;
\frac{p}{q}
\;=\;
r,
\]
so \(r\in S\).

In either case we have shown \(r\in S\).  By the principle of strong induction on \(\mu(r)\), it follows that every rational \(r\in(0,1)\) belongs to \(S\), as required.

problem:
Problem 10.2 The incircle of an isosceles $\triangle A B C$ touches the legs $A C$ and $B C$ at points $M$ and $N$ respectively. A tangent $t$ is drawn to the smaller of the $\operatorname{arcs} \hat{M N}$ and let $t$ intersect $N C$ and $M C$ at points $P$ and $Q$ respectively. Let $T$ be the intersection point of the lines $A P$ and $B Q$.
a) Prove that $T$ lies on the segment $M N$;
b) Prove that the sum of the areas of triangles $A T Q$ and $B T P$ is the smallest possible when $t$ is parallel to $A B$.

solution:
Let \(n\) be a positive integer and \(a_{1},\dots,a_{n}\) positive real numbers.  Define
\[
g(x)\;=\;\prod_{i=1}^{n}(x+a_{i})
\;=\;\sum_{k=0}^{n}e_{k}\,x^{\,n-k},
\]
where for \(0\le k\le n\),
\[
e_{k}
\;=\;
\sum_{1\le i_{1}<\cdots<i_{k}\le n}
a_{i_{1}}a_{i_{2}}\cdots a_{i_{k}}
\]
is the \(k\)th elementary symmetric sum of \(a_{1},\dots,a_{n}\).  In particular
\[
e_{0}=1,
\qquad
e_{1}=a_{1}+\cdots+a_{n},
\]
and since each \(a_{i}>0\), we have \(e_{k}>0\) for all \(1\le k\le n\).

Let \(a_{0}\) be a real number and set
\[
f(x)
=\bigl(x-a_{0}\bigr)\,g(x)
=x^{n+1}+b_{1}x^{n}+b_{2}x^{n-1}+\cdots+b_{n}x+b_{n+1}.
\]
A straightforward multiplication shows that for \(1\le k\le n\),
\[
b_{k}
=\;e_{k}\;-\;a_{0}\,e_{\,k-1},
\]
while
\[
b_{n+1}=-\,a_{0}\,e_{n}.
\]
We will prove:

(⇒) If \(a_{0}>a_{1}+\cdots+a_{n}\), then all \(b_{1},\dots,b_{n+1}\) are negative.

(⇐) If all \(b_{1},\dots,b_{n+1}\) are negative, then \(a_{0}>a_{1}+\cdots+a_{n}\).

---

Proof of “(⇒)”.  Set \(S=e_{1}=a_{1}+\cdots+a_{n}\), and suppose
\[
a_{0}>S.
\]
Then
\[
b_{1}=e_{1}-a_{0}=S-a_{0}<0,
\]
and
\[
b_{n+1}=-\,a_{0}\,e_{n}<0
\]
because \(e_{n}=a_{1}\cdots a_{n}>0\) and \(a_{0}>0\).  It remains to show \(b_{k}<0\) for \(2\le k\le n\).  We shall use the following combinatorial lemma.

Lemma.  For each integer \(k\) with \(1\le k\le n\),
\[
e_{k}
\;\le\;
\frac{e_{1}}{k}\;e_{\,k-1}.
\]

Proof of Lemma.  Fix \(k\in\{1,\dots,n\}\).  For any subset 
\(\;U\subseteq\{1,2,\dots,n\}\) with \(|U|=k\), observe that
\[
\sum_{j\in U}
a_{j}\;\prod_{\,i\in U\setminus\{j\}}a_{i}
\;=\;
k\;\prod_{\,i\in U}a_{i},
\]
because the left sum has \(k\) identical terms each equal to \(\prod_{i\in U}a_{i}\).  Hence
\[
\prod_{i\in U}a_{i}
\;=\;
\frac{1}{k}
\sum_{j\in U}
a_{j}\;\prod_{i\in U\setminus\{j\}}a_{i}.
\]
Summing this identity over all \(\binom{n}{k}\) subsets \(U\) of size \(k\) gives
\[
e_{k}
=\sum_{\substack{U\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}\\|U|=k}}
\prod_{i\in U}a_{i}
=\frac{1}{k}
\sum_{\substack{U\\|U|=k}}
\sum_{j\in U}
a_{j}\;\prod_{i\in U\setminus\{j\}}a_{i}.
\]
Re‐index the double sum by first choosing \(j\in\{1,\dots,n\}\) and then choosing a \((k-1)\)-subset \(T\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}\setminus\{j\}\).  That is,
\[
\sum_{\substack{U\\|U|=k}}
\sum_{j\in U}
a_{j}\prod_{i\in U\setminus\{j\}}a_{i}
\;=\;
\sum_{j=1}^{n}
\;a_{j}\;
\sum_{\substack{T\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}\setminus\{j\}\\|T|=k-1}}
\prod_{i\in T}a_{i}.
\]
But for each fixed \(j\), the inner sum is precisely the \((k-1)\)st elementary symmetric sum of the \(n-1\) numbers \(\{a_{i}:i\neq j\}\), which is at most
\[
\sum_{\substack{T\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}\\|T|=k-1}}
\prod_{i\in T}a_{i}
\;=\;
e_{\,k-1}.
\]
Thus
\[
\sum_{\substack{U\\|U|=k}}
\sum_{j\in U}
a_{j}\prod_{i\in U\setminus\{j\}}a_{i}
\;\le\;
\sum_{j=1}^{n}a_{j}\;e_{\,k-1}
\;=\;
e_{1}\,e_{\,k-1},
\]
and consequently
\[
e_{k}
=\frac{1}{k}
\sum_{\substack{U\\|U|=k}}
\sum_{j\in U}
a_{j}\prod_{i\in U\setminus\{j\}}a_{i}
\;\le\;
\frac{e_{1}}{k}\;e_{\,k-1},
\]
proving the lemma.  ▭

Returning to the main proof, for each \(2\le k\le n\) we now have
\[
b_{k}
=e_{k}-a_{0}\,e_{\,k-1}
\;\le\;
\frac{e_{1}}{k}\,e_{\,k-1}\;-\;a_{0}\,e_{\,k-1}
\;=\;
e_{\,k-1}\Bigl(\frac{e_{1}}{k}-a_{0}\Bigr).
\]
Since \(a_{0}>e_{1}\ge e_{1}/k\), the factor in parentheses is negative, and \(e_{\,k-1}>0\), so \(b_{k}<0\).  Hence under the hypothesis \(a_{0}>a_{1}+\cdots+a_{n}\) all coefficients \(b_{1},\dots,b_{n+1}\) are indeed negative.

---

Proof of “(⇐)”.  Conversely, if all \(b_{1},\dots,b_{n+1}\) are negative then in particular
\[
b_{1}
\;=\;
e_{1}-a_{0}
\;<\;
0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
a_{0}>e_{1}=a_{1}+\cdots+a_{n}.
\]
This is exactly the required condition.  \(\quad\)

Define new variables by \(x=\tfrac1a,\;y=\tfrac1b,\;z=\tfrac1c\).  Since \(a,b,c>0\), we have \(x,y,z>0\), and the condition
\[
ab+bc+ca\;\le\;3abc
\]
is equivalent to
\[
\frac1{xy}+\frac1{yz}+\frac1{zx}\;\le\;\frac3{xyz}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
x+y+z\;\le\;3.
\]
Moreover a short computation shows
\[
\sqrt{\frac{a^2+b^2}{a+b}}
=\sqrt{\frac{\frac1{x^2}+\frac1{y^2}}{\frac1x+\frac1y}}
=\sqrt{\frac{x^2+y^2}{\,xy\,(x+y)\,}},
\qquad
\sqrt{a+b}=\sqrt{\frac{x+y}{xy}}.
\]
Hence the original inequality
\[
\sqrt{\frac{a^2 + b^2}{a+b}}
+\sqrt{\frac{b^2 + c^2}{b+c}}
+\sqrt{\frac{c^2 + a^2}{c+a}}
+3
\;\le\;
\sqrt2\;\bigl(\sqrt{a+b}+\sqrt{b+c}+\sqrt{c+a}\bigr)
\]
becomes, after substituting and clearing notation,
\[
\sum_{\rm cyc}\sqrt{\frac{x^2+y^2}{\,xy\,(x+y)\,}}\;+\;3
\;\le\;
\sqrt2\;\sum_{\rm cyc}\sqrt{\frac{x+y}{xy}}\,,
\]
or equivalently
\[
\sqrt2\sum_{\rm cyc}\sqrt{\frac{x+y}{xy}}
\;-\;\sum_{\rm cyc}\sqrt{\frac{x^2+y^2}{\,xy\,(x+y)\,}}
\;\ge\;3.
\tag{1}
\]
We shall prove (1) by two steps.

Step 1.  Termwise estimate.  Fix any positive \(t,u\).  We claim
\[
\sqrt{\frac{2t}{u}}
\;-\;
\sqrt{\frac{t^2-2u}{\,u\,t\,}}
\;\ge\;
\sqrt{\frac{2}{t}}.
\tag{2}
\]
Indeed, set
\[
A=\sqrt{\frac{2t}{u}},\quad
B=\sqrt{\frac{t^2-2u}{\,u\,t\,}},\quad
C=\sqrt{\frac{2}{t}}.
\]
We must show \(A-B\ge C\).  Equivalently,
\[
A\;\ge\;B+C.
\]
Squaring both sides (all quantities are nonnegative) gives
\[
\frac{2t}{u}
\;\ge\;
\frac{t^2-2u}{u\,t}
\;+\;\frac{2}{t}
\;+\;
2\sqrt{\frac{t^2-2u}{u\,t}\,\cdot\,\frac{2}{t}}
\;.
\]
Observe
\[
\frac{t^2-2u}{u\,t}+\frac{2}{t}
=\frac{t^2-2u+2u}{u\,t}
=\frac{t^2}{u\,t}
=\frac{t}{u},
\]
so the inequality becomes
\[
\frac{2t}{u}\;\ge\;\frac{t}{u}
\;+\;
2\sqrt{\frac{2(t^2-2u)}{u\,t^2}}
\;\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\frac{t}{u}
\;\ge\;
2\sqrt{\frac{2(t^2-2u)}{u\,t^2}}.
\]
Squaring again yields
\[
\frac{t^2}{u^2}
\;\ge\;
4\,\frac{2(t^2-2u)}{u\,t^2}
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
t^4
\;\ge\;
8u\,t^2-16u^2
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
(t^2-4u)^2\;\ge\;0,
\]
which is manifestly true.  Hence (2) holds for all \(t,u>0\).

Now apply (2) to each cyclic pair \((x,y)\) by setting \(t=x+y\), \(u=xy\).  Since
\[
\sqrt{\frac{x^2+y^2}{\,xy\,(x+y)\,}}
=\sqrt{\frac{t^2-2u}{u\,t}}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\sqrt{\frac{x+y}{xy}}
=\sqrt{\frac{t}{u}},
\]
inequality (2) reads
\[
\sqrt2\,\sqrt{\frac{x+y}{xy}}
\;-\;
\sqrt{\frac{x^2+y^2}{\,xy\,(x+y)\,}}
\;\ge\;
\sqrt{\frac{2}{\,x+y\,}}.
\]
Summing this cyclically over \((x,y,z)\) gives
\[
\sqrt2\sum_{\rm cyc}\sqrt{\frac{x+y}{xy}}
\;-\;
\sum_{\rm cyc}\sqrt{\frac{x^2+y^2}{\,xy\,(x+y)\,}}
\;\ge\;
\sum_{\rm cyc}\sqrt{\frac{2}{\,x+y\,}}
=\sqrt2\sum_{\rm cyc}\frac1{\sqrt{x+y}}.
\]
Hence to prove (1) it suffices to show
\[
\sum_{\rm cyc}\frac1{\sqrt{x+y}}
\;\ge\;\frac3{\sqrt2}.
\tag{3}
\]

Step 2.  Jensen’s inequality.  The function \(f(t)=1/\sqrt t\) is convex on \((0,\infty)\).  By Jensen’s inequality,
\[
\frac{1}{3}\Bigl[f(x+y)+f(y+z)+f(z+x)\Bigr]
\;\ge\;
f\!\Bigl(\frac{(x+y)+(y+z)+(z+x)}{3}\Bigr)
\;=\;
f\!\Bigl(\tfrac{2(x+y+z)}3\Bigr).
\]
Since \(x+y+z\le3\), we have \(\tfrac{2(x+y+z)}3\le2\), and \(f\) is decreasing, so
\[
f\!\Bigl(\tfrac{2(x+y+z)}3\Bigr)
\;\ge\;
f(2)
\;=\;
\frac1{\sqrt2}.
\]
Hence
\[
\frac{1}{3}\sum_{\rm cyc}\frac1{\sqrt{x+y}}
\;\ge\;\frac1{\sqrt2}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\sum_{\rm cyc}\frac1{\sqrt{x+y}}
\;\ge\;\frac{3}{\sqrt2},
\]
which is exactly (3).

Combining the two steps, we obtain
\[
\sqrt2\sum\sqrt{\frac{x+y}{xy}}
\;-\;
\sum\sqrt{\frac{x^2+y^2}{xy\,(x+y)}}
\;\ge\;
\sum\sqrt{\frac{2}{x+y}}
\;\ge\;3,
\]
i.e.\ (1).  Reverting to the original variables \(a,b,c\) completes the proof of the desired inequality.

Let \(O_{1},O_{2}\) be the centers of \(\omega_{1},\omega_{2}\) with radii \(r_{1},r_{2}\), respectively, and let \(O\) be the center of the circle \(\Gamma\) through \(P,Q,R,S\) with radius \(R\).  Denote \(d = O_{1}O_{2}\).

1.  Recall that for any circle with center \(C\) and radius \(\rho\), the power of a point \(Z\) with respect to that circle is
\[
  \Pow(Z) \;=\; ZC^{2} \;-\;\rho^{2}.
\]
   Also, if two circles intersect, the line through their two intersection points is their radical axis, and a point on the radical axis has equal power with respect to both circles.

2.  Since \(P\) and \(Q\) lie on both \(\Gamma\) and \(\omega_{2}\), the line \(PQ\) is the radical axis of \(\Gamma\) and \(\omega_{2}\).  But by construction \(\ell_{1}\) passes through \(O_{1}\) and contains \(P\) and \(Q\), so \(O_{1}\) lies on the radical axis \(PQ\).  Hence
\[
  \Pow_{\Gamma}(O_{1}) \;=\;\Pow_{\omega_{2}}(O_{1}).
\]
  Writing each side in center–radius form,
\[
  O_{1}O^{2} \;-\;R^{2}
  \;=\;
  O_{1}O_{2}^{2} \;-\;r_{2}^{2}
  \;=\;
  d^{2} \;-\; r_{2}^{2}.
\]
  Therefore
\[
  O_{1}O^{2} \;=\; R^{2} \;+\; d^{2} \;-\; r_{2}^{2}.
  \tag{1}
\]

3.  Likewise, \(R\) and \(S\) lie on both \(\Gamma\) and \(\omega_{1}\), so \(RS\) is the radical axis of \(\Gamma\) and \(\omega_{1}\), and it contains \(O_{2}\).  Thus
\[
  \Pow_{\Gamma}(O_{2}) \;=\;\Pow_{\omega_{1}}(O_{2}),
\]
  i.e.
\[
  O_{2}O^{2} \;-\; R^{2}
  \;=\;
  O_{2}O_{1}^{2} \;-\; r_{1}^{2}
  \;=\;
  d^{2} \;-\; r_{1}^{2},
\]
  and hence
\[
  O_{2}O^{2} \;=\; R^{2} \;+\; d^{2} \;-\; r_{1}^{2}.
  \tag{2}
\]

4.  Finally, we check the power of \(O\) with respect to \(\omega_{1}\) and \(\omega_{2}\):
\[
  \Pow_{\omega_{1}}(O)
  \;=\;
  OO_{1}^{2} \;-\; r_{1}^{2}
  \;\overset{(1)}=\;
  \bigl(R^{2}+d^{2}-r_{2}^{2}\bigr) \;-\; r_{1}^{2}
  \;=\;
  R^{2}+d^{2}-(r_{1}^{2}+r_{2}^{2}),
\]
\[
  \Pow_{\omega_{2}}(O)
  \;=\;
  OO_{2}^{2} \;-\; r_{2}^{2}
  \;\overset{(2)}=\;
  \bigl(R^{2}+d^{2}-r_{1}^{2}\bigr) \;-\; r_{2}^{2}
  \;=\;
  R^{2}+d^{2}-(r_{1}^{2}+r_{2}^{2}).
\]
Hence
\[
  \Pow_{\omega_{1}}(O)
  \;=\;
  \Pow_{\omega_{2}}(O),
\]
which means \(O\) lies on the radical axis of \(\omega_{1}\) and \(\omega_{2}\).  Since these two circles intersect at \(X\) and \(Y\), their radical axis is the line \(XY\).  Therefore the center \(O\) of the circle through \(P,Q,R,S\) lies on \(XY\), as required.

