solution:
All denominators which appear are written explicitly, and every algebraic step is justified.  We only use the following well‐known facts:

  •  The identity  
     \[
       X^{p-1}-1 \;=\;\prod_{a\in\F_p^\times}(X-a)
     \]
     over the finite field \(\F_p\) (a direct corollary of Fermat’s little theorem).

  •  The elementary‐symmetric‐polynomial expansion
     \[
       \prod_{i=0}^{N-1}(t + x_i)
       \;=\;
       \sum_{k=0}^N e_k(x_0,\dots,x_{N-1})\;t^{\,N-k}\,,
     \]
     where
     \(\displaystyle
       e_k(x_0,\dots,x_{N-1})
       =\sum_{0\le i_1<\cdots<i_k\le N-1}x_{i_1}\cdots x_{i_k}.
     \)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Proof.  Fix a prime \(p\), and let \(1<d\le p\).  We proceed in two steps.

Step 1.  (A closed‐form for \(G(d,n)\).)  
We claim that for every integer \(n\ge d\ge1\),
\[
   G(d,n)
   \;=\;
   \frac{d!}{\,n\,}
   \;\sum_{1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-1}\le n-1}
     \frac{1}{\,i_1\,i_2\cdots i_{d-1}\,}.
\tag{1}
\]
We prove this by induction on \(d\).

Base case \(d=1\).  By definition \(G(1,n)=1/n\).  On the right of \((1)\) the sum over an “empty” tuple \((i_1,\dots,i_0)\) is by convention \(1\), so the right‐hand side is
\[
   \frac{1!}{n}\;\times1
   \;=\;\frac1n
   \;=\;G(1,n).
\]

Inductive step.  Suppose \((1)\) holds for \(d-1\).  Then by the recursive definition
\[
  G(d,n)
  \;=\;
  \frac{d}{n}\,
  \sum_{k=d}^{n}G(d-1,k-1).
\]
By the induction hypothesis,
\[
  G(d-1,k-1)
  \;=\;
  \frac{(d-1)!}{\,k-1\,}
  \sum_{1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-2}\le k-2}
    \frac{1}{i_1\cdots i_{d-2}}.
\]
Hence
\[
  G(d,n)
  =\,
  \frac{d}{n}
  \sum_{k=d}^{n}
    \Biggl[
      \frac{(d-1)!}{k-1}
      \sum_{1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-2}\le k-2}
        \frac1{i_1\cdots i_{d-2}}
    \Biggr].
\]
Set \(m=k-1\); then \(m\) runs from \(d-1\) to \(n-1\), and
\[
  G(d,n)
  =
  \frac{d\,(d-1)!}{n}
  \sum_{m=d-1}^{n-1}
    \frac1m
    \sum_{1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-2}\le m-1}
      \frac1{i_1\cdots i_{d-2}}
  \;=\;
  \frac{d!}{n}
  \sum_{m=d-1}^{n-1}
    \sum_{1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-2}\le m-1}
      \frac1{\,m\,i_1\cdots i_{d-2}\,}.
\]
In the double sum we first choose
\((i_1,\dots,i_{d-2})\) with \(1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-2}\le n-2\), and then choose
\(m\) with \(i_{d-2}+1\le m\le n-1\).  Thus
\[
  \sum_{m=d-1}^{n-1}
    \sum_{1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-2}\le m-1}
      \frac1{\,m\,i_1\cdots i_{d-2}\,}
  =
  \sum_{1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-2}\le n-2}
    \sum_{m=i_{d-2}+1}^{n-1}
      \frac1{\,m\,i_1\cdots i_{d-2}\,}
  =
  \sum_{1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-1}\le n-1}
    \frac1{\,i_1\cdots i_{d-1}\,}.
\]
Putting this back into the formula for \(G(d,n)\) proves \((1)\) for all \(d,n\).

Step 2.  (The denominator of \(G(d,p)\) is prime‐to \(p\).)  
Specialize \((1)\) to \(n=p\).  Since \(d\le p\) we have \(\gcd(d!,p)=1\).  Thus
\[
  G(d,p)
  =
  \frac{d!}{\,p\,}
  \;\sum_{1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-1}\le p-1}
    \frac1{i_1\cdots i_{d-1}}.
\]
Write
\[
  T \;:=\;
  \sum_{1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-1}\le p-1}
    \frac1{i_1\cdots i_{d-1}}.
\]
To show that the reduced denominator of \(G(d,p)\) is not divisible by \(p\) it suffices to show that \(T\) is divisible by \(p\).  Equivalently, working in the finite field \(\F_p\), we must show
\[
   T \;=\;
   \sum_{1\le i_1<\cdots<i_{d-1}\le p-1}
     \frac1{i_1\cdots i_{d-1}}
   \;=\;0
   \quad\text{in }\F_p.
\]
But in \(\F_p\) every \(i_j\) with \(1\le i_j\le p-1\) is invertible, so \(i_j^{-1}\in\F_p^\times\).  Hence
\[
   T
   \;=\;
   \sum_{\substack{S\subset\F_p^\times\\|S|=d-1}}
     \;\prod_{a\in S}a^{-1}
   \;=\;
   \sum_{\substack{S\subset\F_p^\times\\|S|=d-1}}
     \Bigl(\prod_{a\in S}a\Bigr)^{-1}.
\]
Fix once and for all a generator \(g\) of the cyclic group \(\F_p^\times\).  Then the elements of \(\F_p^\times\) are
\(\{g^0,g^1,\dots,g^{p-2}\}\).  Setting \(x_j:=g^{-\,j}\) for \(j=0,1,\dots,p-2\), we see that
\[
   T
   \;=\;
   \sum_{0\le j_1<\cdots<j_{d-1}\le p-2}
     x_{j_1}\,x_{j_2}\cdots x_{j_{d-1}}
   \;=\;
   e_{\,d-1}(x_0,x_1,\dots,x_{p-2}),
\]
the \((d-1)\)th elementary symmetric polynomial in the \(p-1\) variables \(x_0,\dots,x_{p-2}\).

On the other hand, by Fermat’s little theorem the monic polynomial
\(\;X^{p-1}-1\)\(\in\F_p[X]\) has exactly the \(p-1\) distinct roots \(g^0,g^1,\dots,g^{p-2}\), so
\[
   X^{p-1}-1
   \;=\;
   \prod_{a\in\F_p^\times}(X - a)
   \;=\;
   \prod_{j=0}^{p-2}\bigl(X - g^j\bigr).
\]
Replacing \(X\mapsto -\,t\) gives
\[
   t^{p-1}-1
   \;=\;
   \prod_{j=0}^{p-2}\bigl(t + x_j\bigr)
   \;=\;
   \sum_{k=0}^{p-1}
     e_k(x_0,\dots,x_{p-2})\;t^{\,p-1-k}.
\]
But the left‐hand side is \(t^{p-1}-1\), whose coefficient of \(t^{p-1-(d-1)}=t^{p-d}\) is zero whenever \(1\le d-1\le p-2\).  Hence
\[
   e_{\,d-1}(x_0,\dots,x_{p-2})
   \;=\;
   0
   \quad\text{in }\F_p.
\]
Thus \(T=0\) in \(\F_p\), i.e.\ \(p\) divides the numerator of \(T\) (when written in lowest terms).  Since \(d!\) carries no factor of \(p\), the factor \(1/p\) in
\(\;G(d,p)=\tfrac{d!}{p}\,T\)
is exactly cancelled in the reduced form.  In particular the reduced denominator of \(G(d,p)\) is not divisible by \(p\), as claimed.

First Solution.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{k=1}^{n}\binom{n}{k} k^{2} & =\sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{n!}{(n-k)!k!}[k(k-1)+k] \\
& =\sum_{k=2}^{n} \frac{n(n-1)(n-2)!}{(n-k)!(k-2)!}+\sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{n(n-1)!}{(n-k)!(k-1)!}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
& =n(n-1) \sum_{0}^{n} \sum_{j}^{2}\binom{n-2}{j}+n \sum_{i-0}^{n}\binom{n-1}{j} \\
& =n(n-1) 2^{n 2}+n 2^{n} 1=n(n+1) 2^{n 2} .
\end{aligned}
$$

Second Solution. If we apply the operator $x(d / d x)$ to

$$
(1+x)^{n}=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k} x^{k}
$$
we obtain the identity
$$
n x(1+x)^{n-1}=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\binom{n}{k} k x^{k}
$$

Applying it again, we find

$$
n(n-1) x^{2}(1+x)^{n-2}+n x(1+x)^{n-1}=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\binom{n}{k} k^{2} x^{k}
$$

Put $x=1$ and we have

$$
\sum_{k=1}^{n}\binom{n}{k} k^{2}=n(n-1) 2^{n-2}+n 2^{n-1}=n(n+1) 2^{n-2}
$$

Let \(n\) be a fixed positive integer.  Recall the Binomial Theorem: for all real \(x\),  
\[
(1 + x)^n \;=\; \sum_{k=0}^n \binom{n}{k}\,x^k.
\]  
Since the right–hand side is a polynomial in \(x\), we may differentiate term by term any number of times.  

1.  First derivative.  
   Differentiate both sides with respect to \(x\).  On the left we get
   \[
     \frac{d}{dx}\bigl[(1+x)^n\bigr]
     \;=\; n\,(1+x)^{n-1}.
   \]
   On the right,
   \[
     \frac{d}{dx}\Bigl[\sum_{k=0}^n \binom{n}{k}x^k\Bigr]
     \;=\; \sum_{k=0}^n \binom{n}{k}\,\frac{d}{dx}(x^k)
     \;=\; \sum_{k=0}^n \binom{n}{k}\,k\,x^{k-1}.
   \]
   Hence for all \(x\),
   \[
     n\,(1+x)^{n-1}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^n k\,\binom{n}{k}\,x^{k-1}.
   \]
   Multiply both sides by \(x\):
   \[
     n\,x\,(1+x)^{n-1}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^n k\,\binom{n}{k}\,x^k.
   \]
   Now set \(x=1\).  Since \((1+1)^{n-1}=2^{n-1}\), we obtain
   \[
     \sum_{k=0}^n k\,\binom{n}{k}
     \;=\;
     n\cdot1\cdot2^{n-1}
     \;=\;
     n\,2^{n-1}.
   \]
   Because the term \(k=0\) vanishes, this is equivalently
   \[
     \sum_{k=1}^n k\,\binom{n}{k}
     \;=\;
     n\,2^{n-1}.
     \tag{1}
   \]

2.  Second derivative.  
   Differentiate the identity
   \(\displaystyle n\,(1+x)^{n-1}=\sum_{k=0}^n k\binom{n}{k}x^{k-1}\)
   once more with respect to \(x\).  On the left,
   \[
     \frac{d}{dx}\bigl[n\,(1+x)^{n-1}\bigr]
     \;=\;
     n\,(n-1)\,(1+x)^{n-2}.
   \]
   On the right,
   \[
     \frac{d}{dx}\Bigl[\sum_{k=0}^n k\binom{n}{k}x^{k-1}\Bigr]
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^n k\binom{n}{k}\,(k-1)\,x^{k-2}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^n k(k-1)\binom{n}{k}\,x^{k-2}.
   \]
   Hence
   \[
     n(n-1)\,(1+x)^{n-2}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^n k(k-1)\binom{n}{k}\,x^{k-2}.
   \]
   Multiply both sides by \(x^2\):
   \[
     n(n-1)\,x^2\,(1+x)^{n-2}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^n k(k-1)\binom{n}{k}\,x^k.
   \]
   Setting \(x=1\) (so \((1+1)^{n-2}=2^{n-2}\)) gives
   \[
     \sum_{k=0}^n k(k-1)\binom{n}{k}
     \;=\;
     n(n-1)\,2^{n-2}.
   \]
   Again the \(k=0\) and \(k=1\) terms vanish, so
   \[
     \sum_{k=2}^n k(k-1)\binom{n}{k}
     \;=\;
     n(n-1)\,2^{n-2}.
     \tag{2}
   \]

3.  Combine to get \(\displaystyle\sum k^2\binom{n}{k}\).  
   Observe the elementary algebraic identity
   \[
     k^2 \;=\; k(k-1) \;+\; k.
   \]
   Therefore
   \[
     \sum_{k=1}^n k^2\binom{n}{k}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=1}^n \bigl[k(k-1)+k\bigr]\binom{n}{k}
     \;=\;
     \underbrace{\sum_{k=1}^n k(k-1)\binom{n}{k}}_{(2)}
     \;+\;
     \underbrace{\sum_{k=1}^n k\binom{n}{k}}_{(1)}.
   \]
   Substituting the values from (1) and (2) yields
   \[
     \sum_{k=1}^n k^2\binom{n}{k}
     \;=\;
     n(n-1)\,2^{n-2}
     \;+\;
     n\,2^{n-1}
     \;=\;
     \bigl[n(n-1)+2n\bigr]\,2^{n-2}
     \;=\;
     n(n+1)\,2^{n-2}.
   \]
This completes the proof that
\[

  \sum_{k=1}^n \binom{n}{k}\,k^2
  \;=\;
  n(n+1)\,2^{\,n-2}.

\]

Fix a positive integer \(n\).  We will show that the sum of the first \(n\) terms in the binomial‐series expansion of \((2-1)^{-n}\) is \(\tfrac12\).  We proceed in two steps:

1.  (Identification of the partial sum.)  
   By Newton’s generalized binomial theorem (see, e.g., “Binomial series” on Wikipedia), for any real exponent \(\alpha\) and any \(x\) with \(|x|<1\) one has
   \[
     (1+x)^{\alpha}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\binom{\alpha}{k}\,x^k,
     \quad
     \binom{\alpha}{k}=\frac{\alpha(\alpha-1)\cdots(\alpha-k+1)}{k!}.
   \]
   Taking \(\alpha=-n\) and \(x=-\tfrac12\) (note that \(\lvert -\tfrac12\rvert<1\)) gives
   \[
     \Bigl(1-\tfrac12\Bigr)^{-n}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\binom{-n}{k}\Bigl(-\tfrac12\Bigr)^k.
   \]
   But \(\bigl(1-\tfrac12\bigr)^{-n}=(\tfrac12)^{-n}=2^n\).  Hence
   \[
     2^n
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\binom{-n}{k}\,(-1)^k\,2^{-k}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\bigl[\binom{-n}{k}(-1)^k\bigr]\,2^{-k}.
   \]
   Therefore
   \[
     (2-1)^{-n}
     \;=\;
     1^ {-n}
     \;=\;
     2^{-n}\,2^n
     \;=\;
     2^{-n}\,
     \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\binom{-n}{k}(-1)^k\,2^{-k}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\binom{-n}{k}(-1)^k\,2^{-n-k}.
   \]
   Finally we use the well‐known identity
   \[
     \binom{-n}{k}
     \;=\;
     \frac{(-n)(-n-1)\cdots(-n-k+1)}{k!}
     \;=\;
     (-1)^k\,\frac{n(n+1)\cdots(n+k-1)}{k!}
     \;=\;
     (-1)^k\binom{n+k-1}{k}.
   \]
   Hence
   \[
     (2-1)^{-n}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
     \bigl[(-1)^k\binom{n+k-1}{k}\bigr]
     (-1)^k\,
     2^{-n-k}
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}
     \binom{n+k-1}{k}\,2^{-\,n-k}.
   \]
   It follows that the sum of the first \(n\) terms (i.e.\ \(k=0,1,\dots,n-1\)) in this expansion is
   \[
     S_n
     \;=\;
     \sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\binom{n+k-1}{k}\,2^{-\,n-k}.
   \]
   We must show \(S_n=\tfrac12\).

2.  (A probabilistic evaluation of \(S_n\).)  
    Consider independent flips of a fair coin, and let each flip be called “Head” (H) or “Tail” (T).  Define the random times
    \[
      H_n
      = 
      \min\{\,m\ge1:\text{the \(m\)–th flip is the \(n\)–th Head}\},
      \quad
      T_n
      = 
      \min\{\,m\ge1:\text{the \(m\)–th flip is the \(n\)–th Tail}\}.
    \]
    We stop flipping as soon as either \(n\) Heads have appeared or \(n\) Tails have appeared.  By symmetry (interchanging H’s and T’s), the probability that the \(n\)–th Head occurs before the \(n\)–th Tail is
    \[
      \Pr(H_n<T_n)
      \;=\;
      \frac12.
    \]
    On the other hand, the event \(\{H_n<T_n\}\) is equivalent to the statement “the \(n\)–th Head occurs by time \(2n-1\),” because if the \(n\)–th Head first appears at some \(m\le2n-1\), then the total number of Tails at time \(m\) is
    \(\,m-(\text{#Heads})=m-n\le(2n-1)-n=n-1< n\),
    so the \(n\)–th Tail has not yet occurred; and conversely if \(H_n>2n-1\), then within the first \(2n-1\) flips there must already be \(n\) Tails, so \(T_n\le2n-1<H_n\).  Hence
    \[
      \{\,H_n<T_n\}
      \;=\;
      \{\,H_n\le2n-1\}.
    \]
    Therefore
    \[
      \Pr(H_n<T_n)
      \;=\;
      \Pr\bigl(H_n\le2n-1\bigr)
      \;=\;
      \sum_{m=n}^{2n-1}\Pr(H_n=m).
    \]
    But it is classical that
    \[
      \Pr(H_n=m)
      \;=\;
      \Pr\bigl(\text{exactly \(n-1\) Heads in the first \(m-1\) flips}\bigr)
      \;\times\;
      \Pr(\text{Head on flip }m)
      \;=\;
      \binom{m-1}{n-1}\Bigl(\tfrac12\Bigr)^{m-1}\!\times\tfrac12
      \;=\;
      \binom{m-1}{n-1}\Bigl(\tfrac12\Bigr)^m.
    \]
    Writing \(m=n+k\), so \(k=0,1,\dots,n-1\), we get
    \[
      \Pr(H_n\le2n-1)
      \;=\;
      \sum_{k=0}^{n-1}
      \binom{(n+k)-1}{n-1}\Bigl(\tfrac12\Bigr)^{\,n+k}
      \;=\;
      \sum_{k=0}^{n-1}
      \binom{n+k-1}{k}\,2^{-\,n-k}
      \;=\;
      S_n.
    \]
    Combining with \(\Pr(H_n<T_n)=\tfrac12\) shows
    \[
      S_n
      \;=\;
      \tfrac12,
    \]
    as claimed.

Conclusion.  The sum of the first \(n\) terms in the binomial expansion of \((2-1)^{-n}\), namely
\[
  \sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\binom{n+k-1}{k}\,2^{-\,n-k},
\]
equals \(\tfrac12\).  This completes the proof.

problem:
Let $C$ be the set of continuous functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$. Let $T : C \to C$ satisfty the following two properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Linearity: $T(af + bg) = aT(f) + bT(g)$ for all $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$ and all $f, g \in C$.
\item Locality: If $f \in C$ and $g \in C$ are identical on some interval $I$, then $T(f)$ and $T(g)$ are also identical on $I$.
\end{enumerate}
Prove that there exists some function $f \in C$ such that $T(g(x)) = f(x)g(x)$ for all $g \in C$.

