solution:
Suppose, to the contrary, that \(\alpha\) is rational.  Write
\[
\alpha=\frac{a}{b},
\]
where \(a,b\) are integers with \(b>0\) and \(\gcd(a,b)=1\).  Define
\[
\zeta \;=\; e^{\,i\pi\alpha}
\;=\;
e^{\,i\pi\,a/b}
\;=\;
e^{\,2\pi i\,a/(2b)}.
\]
Then 
\[
\zeta^{2b}
\;=\;
\bigl(e^{\,i\pi a/b}\bigr)^{2b}
\;=\;
e^{\,2\pi i a}
\;=\;
1,
\]
so \(\zeta\) is a \(2b\)-th root of unity.  Hence \(\zeta\) is a root of the monic polynomial
\[
X^{2b}-1=0
\]
with integer coefficients.  By definition, any complex number which is a root of some monic polynomial in \(\Bbb Z[X]\) is called an algebraic integer.  Thus

1. \(\zeta\) is an algebraic integer.

2. Its inverse \(\zeta^{-1}=e^{-\,i\pi a/b}\) is also a root of the same polynomial \(X^{2b}-1\), hence also an algebraic integer.

3. It is a standard fact (see for instance “Algebraic integer” in any algebraic-number-theory text) that the sum of two algebraic integers is again an algebraic integer.  Therefore
\[
\zeta+\zeta^{-1}
\]
is an algebraic integer.

But a direct computation shows
\[
\zeta+\zeta^{-1}
\;=\;
e^{\,i\pi a/b}+e^{-\,i\pi a/b}
\;=\;
2\cos\!\bigl(\tfrac{\pi a}{b}\bigr)
\;=\;
2\cos(\pi\alpha)
\;=\;
2\cdot\frac13
\;=\;
\frac23.
\]
We have deduced that the rational number \(\tfrac23\) is an algebraic integer.  Finally we invoke the elementary lemma:

Lemma.  If a rational number \(r=\tfrac{p}{q}\) in lowest terms is an algebraic integer, then \(q=\pm1\), i.e.\ \(r\in\Bbb Z\).

Proof of Lemma.  Suppose
\[
r^n + c_{n-1}r^{n-1} + \cdots + c_1r + c_0
\;=\;
0
\]
is a monic polynomial with all \(c_i\in\Bbb Z\).  Multiply by \(q^n\) to get
\[
p^n + c_{n-1}\,p^{n-1}q + \cdots + c_1\,p\,q^{n-1} + c_0\,q^n
\;=\;
0.
\]
All terms except \(p^n\) are divisible by \(q\), so \(q\) divides \(p^n\).  Since \(\gcd(p,q)=1\), it follows \(q\) must be \(\pm1\).  Hence \(r\) is an integer.  \(\Box\)

Applying the Lemma to \(r=\tfrac23\) shows \(\tfrac23\) cannot be an algebraic integer.  This contradiction arises from the assumption that \(\alpha\) is rational.  Therefore \(\alpha\) must be irrational, as required.

A-2 First solution: Let $a$ be an even integer such that $a^{2}+1$ is not prime. (For example, choose $a \equiv 2(\bmod 5)$, so that $a^{2}+1$ is divisible by $5$.) Then we can write $a^{2}+1$ as a difference of squares $x^{2}-b^{2}$, by factoring $a^{2}+1$ as $r s$ with $r \geq s>1$, and setting $x=(r+s) / 2, b=(r-s) / 2$. Finally, put $n=x^{2}-1$, so that $n=a^{2}+b^{2}, n+1=x^{2}$, $n+2=x^{2}+1$.
Second solution: It is well-known that the equation $x^{2}-2 y^{2}=1$ has infinitely many solutions (the socalled "Pell" equation). Thus setting $n=2 y^{2}$ (so that $n=y^{2}+y^{2}, n+1=x^{2}+0^{2}, n+2=x^{2}+1^{2}$ ) yields infinitely many $n$ with the desired property.
Third solution: As in the first solution, it suffices to exhibit $x$ such that $x^{2}-1$ is the sum of two squares. We will take $x=3^{2^{n}}$, and show that $x^{2}-1$ is the sum of two squares by induction on $n$ : if $3^{2^{n}}-1=a^{2}+b^{2}$, then

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left(3^{2^{n+1}}-1\right) & =\left(3^{2^{n}}-1\right)\left(3^{2^{n}}+1\right) \\
& =\left(3^{2^{n-1}} a+b\right)^{2}+\left(a-3^{2^{n-1}} b\right)^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Fourth solution (by Jonathan Weinstein): Let $n=4 k^{4}+$ $4 k^{2}=\left(2 k^{2}\right)^{2}+(2 k)^{2}$ for any integer $k$. Then $n+1=$ $\left(2 k^{2}+1\right)^{2}+0^{2}$ and $n+2=\left(2 k^{2}+1\right)^{2}+1^{2}$.

