Suppose \(\sum_{n=1}^\infty a_n\) converges with each \(a_n>0\).  Then in particular 
\[
a_n\to0,\quad\text{so}\quad\ln(a_n)\to-\infty.
\]
For each \(n\) set
\[
A = \{n:\;a_n\ge e^{-(n+1)}\}, 
\quad
B = \{n:\;a_n< e^{-(n+1)}\}.
\]
Clearly \(\{1,2,3,\dots\}=A\cup B\), so
\[
\sum_{n=1}^\infty a_n^{\frac{n}{n+1}}
\;=\;
\sum_{n\in A}a_n^{\frac{n}{n+1}}
\;+\;
\sum_{n\in B}a_n^{\frac{n}{n+1}}.
\]
We shall show each of these two sums is finite.

1.  Estimate on \(A\).  
If \(n\in A\), then
\[
a_n\;\ge\;e^{-(n+1)}
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\ln(a_n)\;\ge\;-(n+1)
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\frac{\ln(a_n)}{\,n+1}\;\ge\;-1.
\]
Hence
\[
a_n^{\frac1{n+1}}
\;=\;
\exp\!\Bigl(\tfrac{\ln(a_n)}{\,n+1}\Bigr)
\;\ge\;e^{-1},
\]
and therefore
\[
a_n^{\frac{n}{n+1}}
\;=\;
\frac{a_n}{\,a_n^{1/(n+1)}} 
\;\le\;
e\;a_n.
\]
Since \(\sum_n a_n<\infty\), it follows by comparison that
\[
\sum_{n\in A}a_n^{\frac{n}{n+1}}
\;\le\;
e\sum_{n\in A}a_n
\;\le\;
e\sum_{n=1}^\infty a_n
\;<\;\infty.
\]

2.  Estimate on \(B\).  
If \(n\in B\), then
\[
a_n<e^{-(n+1)}
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\ln(a_n)<-(n+1)
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\frac{n}{n+1}\,\ln(a_n)<-n.
\]
Hence
\[
a_n^{\frac{n}{n+1}}
\;=\;
\exp\!\Bigl(\tfrac{n}{n+1}\,\ln(a_n)\Bigr)
\;<\;
e^{-n}.
\]
But \(\sum_{n=1}^\infty e^{-n}\) is a convergent geometric series.  Therefore
\[
\sum_{n\in B}a_n^{\frac{n}{n+1}}
\;\le\;
\sum_{n=1}^\infty e^{-n}
\;<\;\infty.
\]

Combining the two parts,
\[
\sum_{n=1}^\infty a_n^{\frac{n}{n+1}}
\;=\;
\sum_{n\in A}a_n^{\frac{n}{n+1}}
\;+\;
\sum_{n\in B}a_n^{\frac{n}{n+1}}
\;<\;\infty.
\]
Thus \(\sum_{n=1}^\infty a_n^{n/(n+1)}\) converges, as claimed.

